Package: selectSandwich
Type: Package
Title: Zero-Padded Robust Sandwich Covariance After Variable Selection
Version: 0.1.0
Authors@R: person("Analyst", "User", email = "you@example.com", role = c("aut", "cre"))
Description: After a variable-selection procedure such as stepwise selection
    (e.g. via stats::step()), the fitted lm/glm object only contains the
    selected variables. This package computes a "zero-corrected" coefficient
    vector over the full candidate variable set -- selected variables keep
    their estimated coefficient, unselected variables are set to exactly
    zero -- together with a robust (Huber/White sandwich-type)
    variance-covariance matrix for that full-length vector. Because the
    sandwich bread and meat are computed using the *full* design matrix
    (all candidate predictors, selected or not) evaluated at the
    zero-padded coefficient vector, unselected variables receive a
    genuine, typically small but non-zero, variance estimate. This
    reflects the fact that non-selection of a variable in one particular
    fitted model is not certain evidence that its true effect is zero,
    and avoids reporting deterministic zero standard errors for dropped
    variables. The method supports both linear models (lm) and generalized
    linear models (glm), detected automatically from the fitted object.
